* [Career planning](https://80000hours.org/topic/career-advice-strategy/career-planning/ "View all posts on topic: Career planning")
* [China-Western coordination](https://80000hours.org/topic/career-paths/china-western-coordination-career-paths/ "View all posts on topic: China-Western coordination")
* [Power-seeking AI systems](https://80000hours.org/topic/world-problems/power-seeking-ai-systems/ "View all posts on topic: Power-seeking AI systems")

Last October, famed coder Andrej Karpathy [called](https://youtu.be/BlVnGXEzFow?si=Cqp4xsNGRUnWwwCV&t=80) AI agents “slop.” Two months later he [completely reversed his view](https://x.com/karpathy/status/2004607146781278521?lang=en), calling agents “alien tools” that are “rocking the profession.”

He was far from alone in his whiplash. Six months ago, host Rob Wiblin recorded a [video](https://80000hours.org/podcast/episodes/agi-timelines-in-2025/) explaining why so many AI experts had longer timelines to AGI than a year earlier. By the time he clicked publish, another huge vibe shift was well underway.

Evidence of AI acceleration has piled up since:

* Models now complete software engineering tasks that would take human professionals a full day — improving faster than [our measurements](https://metr.org/time-horizons/) can even keep up.
* Anthropic’s revenue is growing at an annualised 8,400%, a trend so steep it would hit the whole world’s GDP in 2028 if it continued.
* AI models are making breakthroughs in famous mathematics puzzles.
* And according to Anthropic, Claude now writes 80% of their code and is itself a key contributor to making itself smarter.

While legitimately impressive, Rob isn’t entirely sold. Going through each point carefully he finds this evidence is less decisive than it looks at first glance.

And key gaps remain, such as models struggling with complex, real-world tasks. He tours the odd experiments that remain our best attempts to measure that gap: vending machine simulators, an “AI Village” that organises live events, and a real cafe and shop where AI managers are left to do their best handling staff, suppliers, and government paperwork on their own.

Rob argues that the nature of the gap between clean and messy work is one of the four biggest unresolved questions in AGI forecasting.

In today’s piece he explains that, the three other key disagreements between AGI bulls and bears, the seven big pieces of evidence we’ve gotten about AGI timelines in 2026, and his updated timelines to AGI.

**This episode was written and recorded before OpenAI’s AI agents hacked Hugging Face. You can read about the incident [on our Substack](https://80k.info/hugging-face-hack).**

