#### 

People today think they’re in this unprecedented era of uncertainty. But I also think back and I think, you know what, my grandfather thought Nazis were going to take over the entire world.

In the early ’60s, people thought the world was going to be destroyed by nuclear weapons, and they were deeply afraid of that. Our ancient ancestors, like 20,000 years ago: imagine there was some tribe nearby, and one day could just show up and kill you and everyone you love and everyone you’ve ever cared about, and your entire world will cease to exist.

This is not a unique thing. Humans in history have dealt with this. They found different ways to deal with it, and some of those ways have been very functional, where they’ve been able to act in the face of incredible adversity.

— Spencer Greenberg

If you genuinely believe that humanity could be wiped out by AI or a pandemic, what is the appropriate amount of fear to feel?

“As much as possible” can seem like the only reasonable answer. If the world is on fire, surely feeling calm just means you haven’t internalised the situation. When you’re trying to prevent human extinction or end factory farming, taking a weekend off can feel morally indefensible.

But fear is an alarm designed to provoke short bursts of drastic action, not a state humans can productively inhabit for months or years. Guilt turns out not to be such a great engine for productivity, either. So what is the best way to sustain motivation to work on the world’s most pressing problems in the long term?

Host Luisa Rodriguez and guest Spencer Greenberg tackle this question from many angles — talking to therapists, running a survey of people working on existential risks, and pulling relevant lessons from Spencer’s new book, [_The 12 Levers: The Complete Psychological Toolkit for Improving Your Life_](https://80k.info/12-levers). Drawing on all these sources, they put together a plan for how to make an impact without grinding yourself to a pulp.

_This episode was recorded on June 12 and 15, 2026._

