#### 

I’d be having a conversation with somebody at a conference workshop and they would say, ‘Everyone at this conference is so worried about what’s going to happen to humans in the post-AGI world. But frankly I’m more worried: what if the monkeys are still running the show?’

And in this case the monkeys refers to the humans… he had this view that AIs in their superintelligent existence were more deserving and more important to preserve a future world for…

I don’t think it’s the majority belief or common belief within the AI labs… but I do think that it makes it very hard to move forward policy action, to move forward the safety conversation.

— Jasmine Sun

Many AI researchers believe mass job displacement is coming — and some even think there’s a chance their technology will kill everyone. But they’re building it anyway. Writer and journalist [Jasmine Sun](https://jasmi.news/) has been documenting why from the inside.

Jasmine describes her work as an “anthropology of disruption.” She’s embedded herself in Silicon Valley’s AI subcultures — attending the parties and conferences, conducting off-the-record interviews — to understand the beliefs of the small group of people shaping this technology.

Some of her findings are unsettling. Asked what advice they’d give a normal 17-year-old, almost every AI researcher said the same thing: “I have no idea… It’s a really scary time. I don’t think there’s going to be a lot of jobs for them left.”

Their motives for building advanced AI are varied: a mix of optimism for humanity, [techno-determinism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological%5Fdeterminism), and a desire to secure their own future in the face of a possible “[permanent underclass](https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/30/opinion/ai-labor-work-force-silicon-valley.html).” A few go even further, actually hoping for a world where machines — rather than humans — are running the show.

When the room can’t even agree on whether humans should stay in control, building a consensus on how to build AI safely gets much harder.

Beyond Silicon Valley, Jasmine’s also tracking the rise of “[AI populists](https://jasmi.news/p/ai-populism),” who see AI as the latest example of corporate elites concentrating their power at the expense of everyone else. In the US, populist sentiment about AI has mostly manifested in protests and votes [against data centres](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/data-center-backlash-signals-a-fight-over-ai-power/). But sometimes, it has escalated into violence: a [molotov cocktail thrown at Sam Altman’s house](https://www.npr.org/2026/04/13/g-s1-117320/openai-sam-altman-molotov-cocktail), and open fire on the home of a [politician who’d backed a data centre](https://www.washingtonpost.com/nation/2026/04/06/data-center-threat-shooting-indianapolis/). Jasmine thinks public anger will keep finding an outlet, one way or another, until people feel like they’ll actually share in AI’s gains.

In this interview with host Zershaaneh Qureshi, Jasmine Sun takes us inside the multifarious factions on AI’s bleeding edge. They also discuss:

* How “doomer” became the lowest-status label in Silicon Valley, and what that means for AI safety
* Why the AI industry’s PR strategy has failed, and what it would take to rebuild public trust
* What’s under the surface of the Chinese public’s much more positive response to AI
* Jasmine’s reasons to be cautiously hopeful: it’s an unusually high-leverage time to work on AI safety, with policymakers and philanthropists hungry for good ideas

